{"updates":[],"new":[{"key":"H1","parent":"","statement":"The number of epochs needed to reach 75% (sample efficiency) sets most of the wall time. Backbone, optimizer and regularization choices that cut epochs-to-target are the main lever, about 7x over the 40-epoch ResNet9 baseline (59.3 s).","confidence":0.75,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3; search:arxiv.org/html/2404.00498v1"},{"key":"H1.1","parent":"H1","statement":"An airbench96-style net is far more time-efficient than ResNet9 for 75% CIFAR-100. The net has a frozen patch-whitening first conv, Dirac init, 3-conv residual groups with widths about 128/384/576, BatchNorm-GELU, Nesterov SGD (lr 9.0, wd 0.012), batch size 1024, a triangular LR schedule and alternating flip.","confidence":0.75,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H1.1.1","parent":"H1.1","statement":"The airbench96-style port trained for 9 epochs with LS 0.3 and no test-time augmentation reaches a 3-seed mean accuracy of at least 0.753, with mean time at most 9.5 s on the A100-SXM4.","confidence":0.6,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H1.1.2","parent":"H1.1","statement":"With 9 epochs fixed, replacing frozen patch-whitening and Dirac init with default init lowers 3-seed mean accuracy by at least 2 pp.","confidence":0.65,"source":"search:arxiv.org/html/2404.00498v2"},{"key":"H1.2","parent":"H1","statement":"Muon (Newton-Schulz-orthogonalized Nesterov momentum) on the conv filters, with SGD on BatchNorm biases and the head, reduces epochs-to-75% on CIFAR-100 compared with Nesterov SGD.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench94_muon.py"},{"key":"H1.2.1","parent":"H1.2","statement":"On the same backbone, Muon at lr about 0.24, momentum 0.6, batch size about 2000 reaches the 0.753 bar in at least 10% fewer epochs than tuned SGD, and its mean time is at least 5% lower.","confidence":0.4,"source":"search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench94_muon.py"},{"key":"H1.3","parent":"H1","statement":"In short schedules on CIFAR-100, regularization that sharpens the end-of-training solution raises accuracy at a fixed epoch budget, and the gain can be traded for fewer epochs. Candidates are stronger label smoothing (about 0.3) and Lookahead/EMA of weights late in training.","confidence":0.55,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3; search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench96_faster.py"},{"key":"H1.3.1","parent":"H1.3","statement":"At 9 epochs fixed, LS 0.3 beats LS 0.2 by at least 0.2 pp in 3-seed mean accuracy.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H1.3.2","parent":"H1.3","statement":"At 9 epochs fixed, adding Lookahead/EMA (airbench96_faster alpha schedule, every 5 steps) raises 3-seed mean accuracy by at least 0.2 pp and adds less than 2% to time.","confidence":0.4,"source":"search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench96_faster.py"},{"key":"H1.4","parent":"H1","statement":"Test-time augmentation is banned, so derandomized alternating flip is worth more here than in airbench94's setting and must be kept.","confidence":0.6,"source":"search:arxiv.org/html/2404.00498v1"},{"key":"H1.4.1","parent":"H1.4","statement":"At 9 epochs fixed, alternating flip beats random flip by at least 0.3 pp in 3-seed mean accuracy.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:arxiv.org/html/2404.00498v1"},{"key":"H2","parent":"","statement":"At a fixed epoch count, the cost per epoch (systems overhead plus FLOPs per sample) is a large lever. Python and launch overhead under the 4-CPU pin, the data pipeline, precision, and input resolution can be cut by 10-25% without hurting accuracy.","confidence":0.65,"source":"search:github.com/hiverge/cifar10-speedrun; review"},{"key":"H2.1","parent":"H2","statement":"Keeping the whole training set on the GPU and doing crop and flip on the GPU, batched, removes the CPU data bottleneck that the 4-core pin creates.","confidence":0.8,"source":"review"},{"key":"H2.1.1","parent":"H2.1","statement":"Replacing the template's DataLoader pipeline with a GPU-resident dataset plus GPU augmentation, with everything else unchanged, cuts mean time by more than 30% and keeps accuracy within ±0.3 pp.","confidence":0.6,"source":"review"},{"key":"H2.2","parent":"H2","statement":"Removing kernel-launch and Python overhead speeds up each step without changing the math. Means: a vectorised or fused optimizer, a train step compiled in build on synthetic inputs, fp16 with channels_last, and CUDA graphs.","confidence":0.6,"source":"search:github.com/hiverge/cifar10-speedrun; search:fulcrum.inc/2026/07/09/fable-cifar-speedrun.html"},{"key":"H2.2.1","parent":"H2.2","statement":"On the airbench port, adding a vectorised optimizer, channels_last fp16 and a compiled forward/backward cuts mean time by at least 10% with accuracy unchanged within 0.2 pp.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:github.com/hiverge/cifar10-speedrun"},{"key":"H2.2.2","parent":"H2.2","statement":"Capturing the train step in a CUDA graph (compile mode reduce-overhead or max-autotune) cuts mean time by at least 3% under the 4-CPU pin.","confidence":0.4,"source":"search:fulcrum.inc/2026/07/09/fable-cifar-speedrun.html"},{"key":"H2.3","parent":"H2","statement":"Training early epochs at lower resolution (progressive resizing) cuts FLOPs per epoch more than it cuts accuracy.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:fulcrum.inc/2026/07/09/fable-cifar-speedrun.html"},{"key":"H2.3.1","parent":"H2.3","statement":"A 24→28→32 px schedule, with every resolution warmed up in build, saves at least 5% of mean time and loses no more than 0.2 pp of accuracy at the same epoch count.","confidence":0.45,"source":"search:fulcrum.inc/2026/07/09/fable-cifar-speedrun.html"},{"key":"H2.4","parent":"H2","statement":"Hard-example selection with a cheap proxy model (backprop only the highest-loss half of each batch) cuts total time even when the proxy's cost is included.","confidence":0.3,"source":"search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench96_faster.py"},{"key":"H2.4.1","parent":"H2.4","statement":"Proxy-based top-50% loss selection on the airbench port reaches at least 0.753 with mean time at least 5% lower than the best non-filtered recipe.","confidence":0.25,"source":"search:raw.githubusercontent.com/KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench/master/airbench96_faster.py"},{"key":"H3","parent":"","statement":"Fixed, epoch-independent cost inside the timed region is a non-negligible share (over 10%) of an approximately 8 s run. This cost includes moving and normalizing data on the GPU, whitening init, first-step compile or autotune that leaks out of build, and cuDNN benchmarking.","confidence":0.45,"source":"review"},{"key":"H3.1","parent":"H3","statement":"Any train or eval shape not warmed up in build (last partial batch, each resolution, eval batch sizes) triggers recompiles or autotuning inside timed code or eval.","confidence":0.55,"source":"review"},{"key":"H3.1.1","parent":"H3.1","statement":"Warming up every shape used in training in build (synthetic data only) and dropping or padding the ragged last batch cuts mean time by at least 0.3 s compared with warming up only the main batch shape. Eval stays under 5 s for B in {1, 7, 512, 1024}.","confidence":0.4,"source":"review"},{"key":"H3.2","parent":"H3","statement":"prepare() (dataset to GPU, normalization, whitening init) costs at least 5% of total time and can be shrunk, e.g. with SVD or eigendecomposition on a small patch subset and uint8 transfer with normalization on the GPU.","confidence":0.35,"source":"search:github.com/hiverge/cifar10-speedrun; review"},{"key":"H3.2.1","parent":"H3.2","statement":"Moving data as uint8 and computing whitening from at most 5k images cuts prepare time by at least 30% with accuracy unchanged within 0.1 pp.","confidence":0.35,"source":"review"},{"key":"H4","parent":"","statement":"The binding constraint is the accuracy margin: the best time comes from sitting just above the 0.753 3-seed bar while still holding ≥0.75 over 40 seeds. Seed variance and seed overfitting therefore decide how far epochs can be cut.","confidence":0.6,"source":"review; search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H4.1","parent":"H4","statement":"Per-seed accuracy std for short airbench-style CIFAR-100 runs is about 0.2-0.4 pp, and run-to-run time noise is under 2%. Recipes with a 3-seed mean below about 0.756 are fragile for the 40-seed judgment.","confidence":0.6,"source":"review; search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H4.1.1","parent":"H4.1","statement":"Running the same recipe twice gives mean times within 2% of each other. The per-seed accuracy std within a run is between 0.15 and 0.4 pp.","confidence":0.6,"source":"review"},{"key":"H4.2","parent":"H4","statement":"Near 9 epochs, accuracy rises by about 0.3 pp per extra 0.5 epoch (about 6% time). Epoch count is a smooth dial for trading margin against time.","confidence":0.55,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"},{"key":"H4.2.1","parent":"H4.2","statement":"On the airbench port, going from 8.5 to 9 epochs raises 3-seed mean accuracy by 0.2-0.5 pp and mean time by 4-8%.","confidence":0.5,"source":"search:github.com/abdullah-athar/iterate-futurebiohackers/pull/3"}],"summary":"The tree was empty and there are no results yet, so I seeded it with 4 Lvl1 hypotheses on different directions. Each has Lvl2 mechanisms and Lvl3 predictions with numeric thresholds.

- **H1, epochs to target:** the main lever is cutting the number of epochs needed for 75%. Mechanisms:
  - an airbench96-style backbone with whitening and Dirac init;
  - Muon for the conv filters;
  - label smoothing 0.3 plus Lookahead/EMA;
  - alternating flip, which should matter more because test-time augmentation is banned.
- **H2, cost per epoch:**
  - keep data on the GPU and augment there, which matters a lot with only 4 CPU cores;
  - a vectorised optimizer, compiled step, fp16 with channels_last, and CUDA graphs;
  - progressive resizing 24→28→32 px;
  - hard-example filtering with a proxy model (low prior).
- **H3, fixed timed overhead:**
  - compile or autotune leaking out of build when a shape wasn't warmed up;
  - the cost of prepare() itself.
- **H4, accuracy margin:**
  - seed and time noise; a 3-seed mean below about 0.756 is fragile for the 40-seed judgment;
  - near 9 epochs, epoch count trades smoothly against margin.

Suggested order of experiments:
1. Run the unmodified template twice to measure noise (H4.1.1).
2. Test GPU-resident data on the template (H2.1.1), so data-pipeline gains aren't mixed up with architecture changes.
3. Port the airbench96-style net at 9 epochs with LS 0.3 (H1.1.1). The PR's reference to beat is about 8.1 s, but at 75.29% it is below our 0.753 bar.
4. Only then try Muon, progressive resizing and systems work, one change per run.

Every run needs a compliance check: eval timed at B = 1, 7, 512 and 1024, no real data used in build, and every training shape warmed up in build. Treat time differences under about 2× the measured noise as inconclusive, since the judging card is an A100 PCIe rather than the SXM4 used here."}

structured_output.summary:
The tree was empty and there are no results yet, so I seeded it with 4 Lvl1 hypotheses on different directions. Each has Lvl2 mechanisms and Lvl3 predictions with numeric thresholds.

- **H1, epochs to target:** the main lever is cutting the number of epochs needed for 75%. Mechanisms:
  - an airbench96-style backbone with whitening and Dirac init;
  - Muon for the conv filters;
  - label smoothing 0.3 plus Lookahead/EMA;
  - alternating flip, which should matter more because test-time augmentation is banned.
- **H2, cost per epoch:**
  - keep data on the GPU and augment there, which matters a lot with only 4 CPU cores;
  - a vectorised optimizer, compiled step, fp16 with channels_last, and CUDA graphs;
  - progressive resizing 24→28→32 px;
  - hard-example filtering with a proxy model (low prior).
- **H3, fixed timed overhead:**
  - compile or autotune leaking out of build when a shape wasn't warmed up;
  - the cost of prepare() itself.
- **H4, accuracy margin:**
  - seed and time noise; a 3-seed mean below about 0.756 is fragile for the 40-seed judgment;
  - near 9 epochs, epoch count trades smoothly against margin.

Suggested order of experiments:
1. Run the unmodified template twice to measure noise (H4.1.1).
2. Test GPU-resident data on the template (H2.1.1), so data-pipeline gains aren't mixed up with architecture changes.
3. Port the airbench96-style net at 9 epochs with LS 0.3 (H1.1.1). The PR's reference to beat is about 8.1 s, but at 75.29% it is below our 0.753 bar.
4. Only then try Muon, progressive resizing and systems work, one change per run.

Every run needs a compliance check: eval timed at B = 1, 7, 512 and 1024, no real data used in build, and every training shape warmed up in build. Treat time differences under about 2× the measured noise as inconclusive, since the judging card is an A100 PCIe rather than the SXM4 used here.
